+  /* --- FX exclusion from the ink composite (critic 11 family 3: "ink on transparent FX" —
+     combat's porcelain-contoured swing band) ---
+     The crease/ink mask is derived from the OPAQUE scene's depth+normal buffers (transparent
+     FX opt out of the normal prepass and write no depth), and the composite stamps that mask
+     over the FINISHED frame — so an additive FX quad (cane ring, trail, flash, flame,
+     sparkle) drawn in front of inked geometry gets the geometry's lines laid across it at
+     full strength. Measured on shots/r11/combat.png: the block-edge ink crosses the pale
+     swing band uninterrupted — rectilinear wall lines, not a band contour — which is the
+     critic's "porcelain dark contour".
+
+     The signal that separates "bright FX in front" from "surface" is scene ALPHA. Opaque
+     toon draws tag alpha = 1 - slyMetal <= 1 (SLY_METAL_TAG), the sky dome writes exactly 1,
+     normal-blended transparency converges toward 1 — and ADDITIVE FX ACCUMULATE alpha upward
+     past 1 (three sets gl.blendFuncSeparate(SRC_ALPHA, ONE, ONE, ONE) for AdditiveBlending,
+     so dst.a grows by src.a per fragment; the scene RT is HalfFloat and does not clamp).
+     Nothing else in this scene can push a pixel's alpha above 1: the shafts blend
+     ONE/ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA (bounded by 1), water/decals/birds are normal or multiply
+     blends. So max(alpha - 1, 0) is the additive-FX coverage of the pixel and exactly 0
+     everywhere else.
+
+     The gate multiplies the ink mask by 1 - clamp(cut * max(alpha - 1, 0), 0, 1): ink fades
+     in proportion to the FX's own accumulated coverage, and every non-FX pixel multiplies by
+     exactly 1.0. Metal pixels (alpha 1 - m < 1) under-gate by m — fail toward KEEPING ink,
+     never toward losing it. 0 = IEEE-exact no-op (clamp(0*x) = 0 and line *= 1.0 is exact).
+     Arms {0, 1} registered in PREREG-fxink.md; do not ship a nonzero value without that
+     seal's frame verdict. */
+  fxInkCut: 0,
+
